A||||11||DJRice||Ball Joint Recommendations||||1||
Z||000000||DJRice||04-19-2001||09:50 PM||||Just got the word that my 87 needs new upper and lower Ball Joints on the front.  The local Sears Auto Center quoted me $55 each  plus the cost of the joint which they dont know yet becase they dont stock them.  <P>I have yet to replace the ball joints in a car.  Is it a pretty rough job?  I dont have a press convienient....<P>Also, is one brand of ball joint better than another?<P>I did some searching, but didnt see anything that came out and said that one brand is better than another.....<P>Advise is appreciated...<P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6||||reg||1
Z||000001||loafer87gt||04-19-2001||10:00 PM||||I replaced mine with Moog adjustable ball joints. They are not much more and have a lifetime warantee. I paid $67 for mine.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||04-19-2001||10:50 PM||||Hey DJ!<BR>It is a fairly straight forward job...<BR>Get a ball joint tool or pickle fork...<BR>Plus, you may need to drill out rivits if they have never been replaced (lowers)...<BR>There are 6 ball joints in the 84-87 Fiero, donno about the 88, but if you can spring it, buy and replace all 6...<BR>Get the best you can afford, too!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||Tigger||04-20-2001||12:36 AM||||I'm in the process of replacing the front ball joints/tie rods on my Fiero...<P>For MOOG stuff alone:<BR>Upper ball joints = $80.00 pair<BR>Lower ball joints = $70.00 pair<BR>Outer Tie rod ends = $50.00 pair<P>You probably don't have to replace the tie rod ends, but if they are worn or not, this is a good time to change those.<P>Replacing the upper ball joints is fairly easy, if they are on with rivits just drill them out or chisel them off.  Replacing the lowers can be a pain since they were welded in from the factory.  Grind away the weld and you probably will need to heat the arm with a torch to knock them out.  You could have a shop press the new joints in for a small fee.  Good idea to have the car aligned if you do any of work on your own.  A Haynes manual covers the steps on jacking the car, jacking up the lower arm, splitting the joint etc.<P>It's not too hard of a job to replace the upper ball joints and tie rod ends, however it might be best to have a shop replace and press in the new lower ball joints for you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||GarethWright||04-20-2001||02:10 AM||||I did the exact same as Trigger about a month or so ago.  Moog parts from Fierostore, got the outer tie rid and upper b-joint no problem, but my lowers were pretty stuck, so I took it to my mechanic to have those done.  The MOOG parts have a few nifty-helpful things too, I liked the Allen-Key bolts that replaced the Socket style bolt on the upper b-joint, I couldnt get those old ones with a wrench Or socket without help.  Supposedly there is a special tool to get the outer tie rod ends on and off, but My wrench didnt open far enough, so I used a pipe wrench(carefully) and that worked easily enough.<BR>Make SURE to get a Full alignment and Toe-In Adjustment when you are done, I had Really bad bump steer all the way from the mechanic to the alignment place, and then none on the way home.  Have fun,<BR>Gareth<P>oops forgot to mention, Autozone and O'Reillys (around here anyway) do free tool rental, just a security deposit, but you get that back when you give the tools back.<p>[This message has been edited by GarethWright (edited 04-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tom88gt||04-20-2001||08:55 AM||||I got Moog ball joints for my 88 at Discount Auto Parts (mostly in the Southeast) for $39 each.  So the 84-87s should be way cheaper.  Lifetime warranty, grease fitting on the bottom (stock, no fitting).  ||||reg||14
Z||000006||Bob Englert||04-20-2001||09:49 AM||||I have done this on several of my Fieros.<P>The 87 lower ball joints are pressed into the lower control arms.  You need a ball joint press to remove them.  I didn't have much luck doing this myself so I removed the lower ball joints and had a local auto machine shop press out the old lower ball joints and control arm bushings and press in new ones.  <P>The upper ball joints are rivited in if they are original.  Drill out the old rivets and you are in business.  <P>I would strongly suggest that you look carefully at the control arm bushings while you are working there.  This would be an excellent time to replace them as well.  It will make a BIG difference in the overall tightness of the front end.<P>I got the front lower ball joints for my 87 GT at AutoZone for about $16 each.  If I remember correctly the uppers are about $26 each and they are the adjustable uppers.  They have worked very well in several of my cars.<P>Rear lower ball joints are riveted to the control arm like the front uppers.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000007||rodmcneill||04-20-2001||10:48 AM||||There is a guy on Ebay right now selling upper and lower ball joints for Fiero's. I am getting uppers and lowers both sides for $67.50 inc. shipping. Does anyone know if the rear lower ball joint is the same part as the front lower ball joint? If it is I may get an extra set for the rear.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Steve Normington||04-20-2001||10:58 AM||||&lt;84-87&gt;<BR>The rear lower ball joints are different from the front lower ball joints. The rears are the "drill out the rivets" to remove and the fronts are the "get a big honkin press" type.<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000009||rodmcneill||04-20-2001||11:13 AM||||Thanks for the info. Steve.||||reg||1
Z||000010||JoeFiero||04-20-2001||04:01 PM||||Sears also told me that I needed new ball joints. I'm curious, I've got a humming sound that comes from the front end when driving straight or turning to the right, I thought this may be a wheel bearing, could it be from the bad ball joints???<P>||||reg||5
Z||000011||Bob Englert||04-20-2001||07:09 PM||||I doubt that ball joints would hum.  Would suspect wheel bearings.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
